# Trust-critical data files. A one-line diff to either can INVERT a published verdict
# (flagged -> trusted): the adjudication ledger is re-applied to the corpus every sync
# cycle, and the verdict store is what agents consume. Require operator review on any
# change so a data-file diff cannot slip through unnoticed in a larger PR.
#
# Pair this file with branch protection: Settings -> Branches -> require review from
# Code Owners on `main`. Without that toggle, CODEOWNERS only auto-requests review.
#
# SCOPE (honest): this governs the PR path only. The PRODUCTION write path is the mini32
# pushback cron, which commits verdicts.json + the ledger and pushes to `main` DIRECTLY (no PR),
# trusted-by-construction via the box's SSH key + git creds - the real trust anchor. So this
# guard + CODEOWNERS defend against a malicious/accidental human PR, not the cron. Enable branch
# protection with admin-bypass (enforce_admins:false) so the direct cron push still lands; a
# naive "include administrators" toggle would wedge the 2-hourly sync.
/data/adjudications.jsonl   @gautamgb
/data/verdicts.json         @gautamgb

# Self-protect the enforcer. The guard runs from the PR HEAD (pull_request trigger), so a PR
# that neuters the script/workflow AND forges a ledger line would run the neutered version and
# pass. Requiring operator review on the guard itself (+ this file) closes that: the two bypass
# paths - forge the data, or disable the check - can no longer both land without a human.
/scripts/check-adjudication-diff.mjs        @gautamgb
/.github/workflows/adjudication-guard.yml   @gautamgb
/.github/CODEOWNERS                          @gautamgb
